  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the kitchen, Max is bored and looking for a distraction. He approaches Chef Victor and curiously asks about the special dish the chef is preparing, but Victor quickly shuts him down. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что тебе надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что вы готовите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А тебе какое дело?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе нечем заняться?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть пять минут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хотел поучиться у вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Иди работай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А можно попробовать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Нет, нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Потому что я так сказал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, я понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тогда иди отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef.' — Built around the key proper name meaning 'chef, boss'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что тебе надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you need?' The key word here means 'what', which Viktor combines with 'for you'. And Max jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что вы готовите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what are you preparing?' Max puts 'what' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А тебе какое дело?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what is it to you?' Viktor uses the casual singular 'you' with 'for you', as close colleagues and friends do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе нечем заняться?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have nothing to do?' In Russian, 'for you' leads into 'to attend to' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Max answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть пять минут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have five minutes.' Max starts with 'me' and pairs it with 'there is' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хотел поучиться у вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wanted to learn from you.' The key word here means 'I', which Max combines with 'wanted'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Иди работай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go work.' Listen for the word meaning 'go' right alongside 'work' in this line. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А можно попробовать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And can I try it?' The first content word means 'may', and 'to taste' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Нет, нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, you cannot.' A compact expression built around 'not allowed' and 'not allowed'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Listen for the word meaning 'why' right alongside 'why' in this line. And Viktor jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Потому что я так сказал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Because I said so.' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'said'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, я понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, I understand.' Notice the word order: 'I' comes first, and 'understood?' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тогда иди отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then go from here.' The key word here means 'back then', which Viktor combines with 'go'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
